  "text": "Rain is a fundamental part of Earth\u2019s natural water cycle, a continuous process that circulates water from the oceans and land into the atmosphere and back again. Understanding how rain forms and how this cycle sustains life requires looking at the interconnected stages of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.\n\nThe process begins with evaporation. Solar energy heats the surface of oceans, lakes, and rivers, causing liquid water to transform into water vapor, an invisible gas. This vapor rises into the atmosphere because it is lighter than the surrounding air. As the vapor ascends, it encounters cooler temperatures at higher altitudes. This cooling causes the water vapor to condense into tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals. These microscopic particles cluster around dust or other particles in the air, forming clouds. When these droplets collide and merge, they grow larger and heavier. Once the water droplets become too heavy for the updrafts to support, they fall to the ground as precipitation, which we recognize as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n\nThis cycle is not merely a meteorological event; it is the engine that drives the health of our planet\u2019s ecosystems. For plants, the water cycle is the source of life. Rainwater penetrates the soil, providing the essential moisture",
